I’m sorry, but I don’t have the ability to pull live weather data or make real‑time API calls. However, you can easily get the hourly forecast for the next 10 days for the coordinates 37.8651 N, 119.5383 W (Yosemite National Park) by using one of the following services:

| Service | How to use it | What you’ll get |
|---------|---------------|-----------------|
| **OpenWeatherMap** | Sign up for a free API key, then call the *One Call API*:<br>`https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/onecall?lat=37.8651&lon=-119.5383&exclude=current,minutely,daily,alerts&units=imperial&appid=YOUR_KEY` | Hourly temperature, wind speed, precipitation probability, and more for up to 48 hours. For 10‑day forecasts you’ll need the *One Call API* with the `daily` field (which gives daily averages) or use the *Forecast 16‑day* endpoint. |
| **WeatherAPI.com** | Get a free key, then request:<br>`https://api.weatherapi.com/v1/forecast.json?key=YOUR_KEY&q=37.8651,-119.5383&days=10&aqi=no&alerts=no` | Hourly data for each day (temperature, wind_kph, precip_mm, etc.) for 10 days. |
| **NOAA Climate Data Online** | Use the NOAA API with the station ID for the nearest weather station (e.g., `GHCND:USC00000000`). | Hourly observations and forecast data, though you may need to combine multiple endpoints. |

### Quick example with WeatherAPI (JavaScript)

```js
const fetch = require('node-fetch');

const apiKey = 'YOUR_API_KEY';
const url = `https://api.weatherapi.com/v1/forecast.json?key=${apiKey}&q=37.8651,-119.5383&days=10&aqi=no&alerts=no`;

fetch(url)
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => {
    data.forecast.forecastday.forEach(day => {
      console.log(`Date: ${day.date}`);
      day.hour.forEach(hour => {
        console.log(`  ${hour.time} - Temp: ${hour.temp_f}°F, Wind: ${hour.wind_kph} kph, Precip: ${hour.precip_mm} mm`);
      });
    });
  })
  .catch(err => console.error(err));
```

Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your actual key. This script will print the hourly temperature, wind speed, and precipitation for each of the next 10 days.

If you need help setting up an API key or parsing the response, let me know!
